# CLI Tools Documentation

This document provides a detailed overview of the **UserInfoCLI** and **IntuneCLI** PowerShell tools, part of the automation portfolio.  
Both tools are **safe for public sharing** and include **demo fallback data** for anyone testing the scripts without a company environment.

---

## **1️⃣ UserInfoCLI**

**Purpose:**  
- Retrieve user information such as name, email, department, and job title  
- Demonstrates Microsoft Graph API integration in a CLI-style tool

**Features:**  
- Accepts **one or more usernames** via the `-UserNames` parameter  
- Nicely formatted console output  
- Falls back to **demo data** if Graph authentication fails  
- Safe for GitHub and public portfolio

**Example Usage:**

```powershell
# Real Graph environment (requires login)
.\UserInfoCLI.ps1 -UserNames "j.doe@company.com","j.smith@company.com"

# Demo fallback mode (safe for portfolio)
.\UserInfoCLI.ps1 -UserNames "j.doe","jj"
Output Example (Demo Mode):

User Info for j.doe
----------------------
Display Name : J.DOE Demo
Email        : j.doe@example.com
Department   : IT
Job Title    : Demo Analyst


2️⃣ IntuneCLI
Purpose:

Retrieve Intune-managed device information for one or more users

Demonstrates Microsoft Graph API integration for device management

Features:

Accepts one or more usernames via the -UserNames parameter
Displays device name, OS, compliance status, and last check-in
Falls back to demo devices for public portfolio use
CLI-style output demonstrates automation skills

Example Usage:

# Real Graph environment (requires login)
.\IntuneCLI.ps1 -UserNames "j.doe@company.com","j.smith@company.com"

# Demo fallback mode (safe for portfolio)
.\IntuneCLI.ps1 -UserNames "j.doe","jj"


Output Example (Demo Mode):

Intune Devices for j.doe
---------------------------
Device Name   : DemoLaptop1
OS            : Windows 11
Compliance    : Compliant
Last Check-in : 12/7/2025 9:30:00 AM
---------------------------
Device Name   : DemoPhone1
OS            : iOS 17
Compliance    : Compliant
Last Check-in : 12/6/2025 12:00:00 PM

Requirements
PowerShell 7+ recommended


Microsoft Graph PowerShell module for real Graph queries:

Install-Module Microsoft.Graph -Scope CurrentUser


Notes
Both tools include demo fallback data, ensuring safe public usage.
Demonstrates real enterprise automation skills:

PowerShell scripting
CLI parameter handling
Microsoft Graph API integration
Multi-user and multi-device support

Safe for portfolio or GitHub sharing; no sensitive company information included.
